nodecode-gameforge is a rewrite of DECRUX9812's DECRUX9812/hermes-plugin-gameforge:

  https://github.com/DECRUX9812/hermes-plugin-gameforge
  commit ee4a07a7fdf26139ae53ad23156b8b275e99743b
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Ritesh Patel (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the offline half of the toolkit, as six tools and a command. The
sound-effect synthesizer is the original's sfxr algorithm carried across --
the same parameter names and ranges, the same 8x supersampled render loop,
the same generators (coin, laser, explosion, powerup, hit, jump, blip) and
their aliases, the same seeded variation, and the four post-processing styles
(retro, modern, crunchy, distant). The music composer keeps the moods, their
modes, progressions and tempos, and their style names, and renders bass,
arpeggio, melody and drums into a loop of exactly the bars asked for. The
pixel-art half keeps the archetype, monster, item, palette, terrain and prop
vocabulary; the masks are this rewrite's, drawn, shaded and outlined from the
same kind of hue-shifted ramp. The tileset is the original's Wang corner set
idea, drawn from one wrapping noise field so the cells meet. The project
layout is the original's: assets/sfx, assets/music, assets/sprites and
assets/tiles under the project, and a gameforge.json ledger at its root
recording each asset's name, kind, files, recipe, creation time and licence,
so a project started by either reads the other's history. The web and Godot
engines keep the scaffold idea -- a page that plays with no build step, and a
Godot 4 project with a scene and a player script -- and status still probes
for godot, unity, blender, unreal, chrome and ffmpeg on PATH, import copies the
ledger's assets in beside a manifest (game.json for web,
assets/manifest.json for godot), and run opens the engine on the project or
names the program it looked for. The exporter keeps the web half of
forge_export: the PWA bundle and the zip made from it, the single
self-contained .html, and the Capacitor project, with the same file layout
under dist and a stored-entry zip written here instead of by a ziplib. The
command and the section replace the plugin's /forge command and its
config_schema.

Left out: the parts that need a hosted service, a heavyweight toolchain, a
browser or a publisher, because a cell that wrote assets offline should not
grow a network dependency to do it -- AI text-to-3D through fal, Tripo and
Meshy (with rigging and preset animations), ElevenLabs prompt-based sound
effects, NPC voice synthesis, the headless-Chrome and Godot playtesters, the
Unity, Blender and Unreal builders and their headless invocation scripts, the
model3d .glb writers with their previews, the pixelize and sheet packers, the
butler push to itch.io and the Godot, Unity and Capacitor platform builds
(capacitor's project is written; building it still needs Node and a mobile
SDK), the Hermes CLI subcommand, the skills folder, and the pre_tool_call
approval hook. Their guidance survives in the tool descriptions and the
(help :gameforge) topic; the assets they would have consumed are the ones
these tools write.
